course: history
lesson: 10
title: Ancient China: paper, silk, the Silk Road and Confucius
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Look around a classroom: paper in your exercise book, a compass in the science cupboard, fireworks on New Year's night, printed books on the shelf. Each of these began in China.
explain: The Chinese learned to make silk from silkworm cocoons and kept the method secret for centuries, so rich people from Rome to Persia paid a fortune for it.
explain: Silk, spices and ideas travelled along the Silk Road, which was not one road but a web of routes across deserts and mountains, walked by camel caravans resting at oasis towns.
explain: About 2,500 years ago the teacher Confucius taught kindness, honesty and respect for parents and teachers, and that rulers should set a good example. His saying "do not do to others what you would not want done to you" is still known everywhere.
fact: Cai Lun, an official of the Han court, improved papermaking about 1,900 years ago, using tree bark, old rags and fishing nets.
fact: The Chinese learned to make silk from the cocoons of silkworms that eat mulberry leaves, and kept the method secret for centuries.
fact: The Silk Road was not one road but a web of trade routes across deserts and mountains between China, Central Asia, Persia and the Mediterranean.
fact: Along the Silk Road travelled not only goods but also ideas, religions and inventions.
fact: Traders travelled in caravans of camels, which can go a long time without drinking, across the Gobi and Taklamakan deserts, resting in oasis towns.
fact: Confucius was a teacher who lived about 2,500 years ago and taught that people should be kind and honest, respect their parents and teachers, and that rulers should set a good example.
fact: A famous saying of Confucius is: do not do to others what you would not want done to you.
fact: The Chinese discovered that a magnetised needle points north and later used compasses for sailing.
fact: Chinese alchemists discovered gunpowder, probably while looking for a medicine for long life; it was first used for fireworks and later in weapons.
fact: The Chinese printed books with carved wooden blocks over 1,100 years ago; the Diamond Sutra of 868 is the oldest dated printed book.
fact: Bi Sheng later made movable type of clay, so Gutenberg did not invent printing but a fast way of printing with metal letters in Europe.
fact: About 600 years ago the admiral Zheng He sailed huge Chinese ships to India, Arabia and East Africa, and brought back gifts including a giraffe.
exercise: Who improved papermaking about 1,900 years ago? | Cai Lun | Confucius | Zheng He | Bi Sheng
exercise: What did Cai Lun use to make paper? | Tree bark, old rags and fishing nets | Clay and wet sand pressed flat and dried | Papyrus reeds brought all the way from Egypt | Silk and gold thread woven tightly together
exercise: What do silkworms eat? | Mulberry leaves | Rice | Cotton | Bamboo
exercise: Why was silk so expensive in Rome? | China kept how to make it secret | Silk grew only on special trees on the Moon | It was made of real gold thread from Persia | Romans hated wearing it and burned it
exercise: What was the Silk Road? | A web of trade routes across Asia | One long, straight paved road with stone milestones | A river made of silk | A wall around China
exercise: Which places did the Silk Road link? | China, Central Asia, Persia and the Mediterranean | Britain and America, across the ocean | Only two big Chinese cities on the Yellow River | Africa and Australia
exercise: How did traders cross the Gobi and Taklamakan deserts? | In camel caravans | In fast cars | On elephants only | By train
exercise: Where did caravans rest in the desert? | In oasis towns | In igloos | On ships | In castles
exercise: What did Confucius teach about rulers? | They should set a good example | They should never be seen | They could do anything they liked | They should be chosen by dice
exercise: Which saying belongs to Confucius? | Do not do to others what you would not want done to you | All roads lead to Rome | I came, I saw, I conquered | Always obey the strongest person in the room, whatever they ask
exercise: Which way does a magnetised needle point? | North | South | Up | Towards the Sun
exercise: What were the Chinese probably looking for when they found gunpowder? | A medicine for long life | A new bright colour of paint for temples | A new kind of sweet for children | A strong glue for sticking paper
exercise: What was gunpowder first used for? | Fireworks | Cooking | Medicine that worked | Painting pictures
exercise: What is the oldest dated printed book? | The Diamond Sutra of 868 | Gutenberg's Bible, printed in Germany | The Epic of Gilgamesh | The Vedas
exercise: Who made movable type of clay? | Bi Sheng | Gutenberg | Cai Lun | Confucius
exercise: Which animal did Zheng He's fleet bring back from Africa? | A giraffe | A kangaroo | A polar bear | A penguin
test: my history channel said Gutenberg invented printing. that right? | No, China printed long before; he made metal-letter printing fast in Europe | Yes, nobody had ever printed anything before him | Yes, and he was really Chinese | No, the Romans printed newspapers long before anyone
test: Your friend draws the Silk Road as one straight motorway. What is wrong? | It was a web of many routes over deserts and mountains | It was actually a long railway with steam trains | It went only inside China and never left it | It was a tunnel that ran under the sea
test: What would happen if a Roman learned the secret of the silkworms? | Silk could be made outside China and get cheaper | Silkworms would stop spinning and die | China would grow richer than ever | Nothing changes, silk was free anyway
test: You are lost at sea with a magnetised needle on a cork in a bowl of water. What can you find? | Which way is north | How deep the sea is | What time it is | Where the nearest fish is
test: Kid in class shoves someone and says "Confucius said be strong". What would Confucius really say? | Do not do to others what you would not want done to you | Push first and ask questions later | Always win every fight you are in | Respect only the strong, and let the weak look after themselves
test: Why could ideas and religions also travel the Silk Road? | Traders talked, met people and carried books | Ideas flew by themselves | The emperor posted them by plane | Nobody ever talked on the road
test: Why do camels suit desert caravans better than horses? | They can go a long time without drinking | They are faster than any horse or car | They can fly over the tallest dunes | They never need any food or water at all
test: People in Zheng He's time had never seen a giraffe. Why bring one home? | It was a wonderful gift from a far land | To use it to pull the emperor's carts | To eat it at a great feast | To guard the emperor's palace door at night
test: Why is paper better than clay tablets for a long letter? | It is light and you can roll or fold it | It lasts longer if it falls into a fire | It is heavier and so much safer | It cannot get wet, even in heavy rain
test: An alchemist wanted to live for ever and found something else. What? | Gunpowder | Paper | Silk | Chocolate
test: In a game you trade silk for horses at an oasis. Which real thing is this most like? | A Silk Road caravan trade | A Viking raid on a monastery | A Roman chariot race | An Olympic game in Greece
exam: Identify the official of the Han court credited with the improvement of papermaking. | Cai Lun | Bi Sheng | Zheng He | Qin Shi Huang
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called Silk Road? | A network of overland trade routes linking China with Persia and the Mediterranean | A single paved highway lying wholly within China | A canal conveying silk from the Yellow River to Rome | A fortification upon the northern frontier
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the history of printing. | Block printing in China preceded Gutenberg's press by several centuries | Printing was entirely unknown prior to Gutenberg's press | Printing was devised in Rome | Printing was devised by Confucius
exam: Ascertain the maxim commonly attributed to Confucius. | Do not do unto others that which you would not have done unto yourself | All roads lead to Rome | Know thyself and nothing in excess | Divide and rule
